// examples/quant/greeks_higher_order — deterministic second- and third-order
// Black-Scholes sensitivities: vanna, volga, charm, veta, speed, zomma, color,
// ultima.
//
// Copyright 2025 STARGA Inc.
// Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.
// Part of the MIND project (Machine Intelligence Native Design).
//
// WHAT THIS DEMONSTRATES, AND WHY IT IS THE STRONGEST CASE IN THE QUANT SET.
// `examples/quant/black_scholes` already prices the determinism claim in a unit
// a risk officer reads without translation: a premium and its five first-order
// Greeks.  This module goes one derivative deeper, to the place where
// floating-point error compounds worst in the entire option-pricing surface.
//
// Every quantity below is a ratio of small DIFFERENCES of transcendentals.
// Catastrophic cancellation is not the edge case here — it is the normal case.
// A desk that hedges vanna and volga is differentiating twice through `exp` and
// `erfc`: the error in the kernel does not merely propagate, it is amplified by
// the differentiation, and then divided by a small number.  Look at what the
// formulas actually are, below: volga carries the factor `d1*d2`, zomma carries
// `(d1*d2 - 1)`, and ultima carries `d1*d2*(1 - d1*d2) + d1^2 + d2^2`.  Each of
// those is a difference of comparable quantities formed from logs and roots.
//
// The practical consequence is the whole argument for this file.  If those two
// derivatives are not reproducible, neither is the hedge they size.  And unlike
// a price — which a trader will eyeball against a screen and a broker quote, and
// which has an independent market check every second — NOBODY CAN EYEBALL A
// WRONG VOLGA.  There is no intuition to catch a third-order sensitivity that
// is off in its sixth digit; there is no market print to compare it against.
// The only thing standing between a wrong volga and a mis-sized book is that the
// number is computed the same way every time, everywhere.
//
// That is why this example exists: it is the clearest demonstration that the
// TRANSCENDENTAL TIER ITSELF has to be deterministic, not merely the arithmetic
// wrapped around it.  A "deterministic" pricing library that calls the host libm
// for `exp` and `erfc` has determinism exactly nowhere on this page.  Vanna and
// volga are where that distinction stops being philosophical.
//
// WHY THIS IS NOT A LIBRARY.  The closed forms are standard and unremarkable
// (Haug 2007 collects them; they are just derivatives of Black-Scholes-Merton).
// Nothing here is a contribution to finance.  The contribution is that every
// transcendental on the path — exp, log, sqrt, erfc — is rebuilt from `+ - * /`
// on the strict scalar path in `std.detmath`, so a third-order Greek is
// reproducible bit-for-bit on a different ISA rather than merely close.
//
// THE CLAIM, STATED EXACTLY.  This module does NOT claim to match any host libm,
// SciPy, or a vendor risk library in the last bits.  It cannot, and it is not
// trying to: a re-derived erfc rounds differently from Cephes, and both are
// within their stated accuracy.  The claim is narrower and harder:
//     the same source, compiled for two different ISAs, produces byte-identical
//     output, with a stated accuracy bound against a 50-digit oracle.
// Accuracy and reproducibility are orthogonal properties.  Determinism is what
// makes an error analysis meaningful, not a substitute for one.
//
// CLOSED FORM, NEVER A FINITE DIFFERENCE.  Every function below is the analytic
// derivative.  A bumped-and-revalued higher-order Greek would inherit several
// prices' worth of rounding AND a step-size choice — and at second order the
// step-size choice dominates the answer, so a finite-difference version of this
// file would be an example about choosing `h`, not an example about
// determinism.  That is the opposite of the point.  (Finite differences were
// used ONCE, offline, at 50 digits, to validate the closed forms below against
// an independent derivation; see the KAT header.  None survives in the code.)
//
// SELF-CONTAINED, AND WHY.  The detmath kernels are duplicated below instead of
// imported.  mindc 0.10.2 cannot carry an f64 across a module boundary (roadmap
// 17.1): an imported `fn f(x: f64) -> f64` types as `(i64) -> i64` at the call
// site and miscompiles silently.  This mirrors `examples/detmath_kat` and
// `examples/quant/black_scholes`.
// deferred: replace the duplicated kernel block with `import std.detmath;` once
// roadmap 17.1 lands — upgrade path: delete the block between the KERNEL BEGIN
// and KERNEL END markers and call the std surface directly.
//
// KERNELS ARE NAMED `dm_*` DELIBERATELY.  A kernel named `exp`, `log` or `erfc`
// collides with the C99 libm name and can be constant-folded by the host
// toolchain at a call site with literal arguments, silently substituting the
// platform's rounding for ours — which is the exact bug this whole example
// exists to rule out.  The `dm_` prefix makes that collision impossible.
//
// NO `[test]` FUNCTIONS, AND WHY.  The mindc 0.10.2 `[test]` interpreter
// mis-evaluates an f64 comparison in a function body (it takes the then-branch
// unconditionally).  Every guarded function here would return through its
// leading `if x != x` guard and every interpreted assertion would pass
// vacuously — a false green.  The native backend evaluates f64 branches
// correctly, so the gate is build-and-run: `mindc run`, exit code 0 means every
// check passed.  The count of failures is the exit code, so a partial failure is
// never rounded to success.
//
// REFERENCES:
//   Black, F. & Scholes, M. (1973). "The Pricing of Options and Corporate
//     Liabilities." Journal of Political Economy 81(3), 637-654.
//   Merton, R. C. (1973). "Theory of Rational Option Pricing." Bell Journal of
//     Economics and Management Science 4(1), 141-183.
//   Haug, E. G. (2007). "The Complete Guide to Option Pricing Formulas", 2nd ed.
//     — the standard collection of the higher-order closed forms used here.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// =========================== KERNEL BEGIN ==================================
// Duplicated verbatim from std/detmath.mind (roadmap 17.1 blocks importing).
// Do not "improve" these: every polynomial is a fixed left-fold Horner
// recurrence in source order.  Reassociating one changes the last bit of every
// price downstream and breaks the cross-substrate hash.

fn absf(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x < 0.0 {
        return 0.0 - x;
    }
    return x;
}

// +inf by byte-identical IEEE overflow; never a synthesised NaN.
fn inf() -> f64 {
    let mut v: f64 = 1.0;
    let mut i: i64 = 0;
    while i < 350 {
        v = v * 10.0;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// 0x7FF8000000000000 — the MIND-canonical quiet NaN, pinned so an
// out-of-domain result is byte-identical (x86 sets the sign bit, ARM does not).
fn canonical_qnan() -> f64 {
    return __mind_bits_to_f64(9221120237041090560);
}

fn rnd_to_i64(x: f64) -> i64 {
    if x >= 0.0 {
        return (x + 0.5) as i64;
    }
    return 0 - ((0.5 - x) as i64);
}

fn det_exp(x: f64) -> f64 {
    let ln2_hi: f64 = 0.6931467056274414;
    let ln2_lo: f64 = 0.00000047493250390941725;
    let inv_ln2: f64 = 1.4426950408889634;
    let k: i64 = rnd_to_i64(x * inv_ln2);
    let kf: f64 = k as f64;
    let r: f64 = (x - kf * ln2_hi) - kf * ln2_lo;
    let mut t: f64 = 0.0000000000007647163731819816;
    t = 0.00000000016059043836821613 + r * t;
    t = 0.00000000208767569878681 + r * t;
    t = 0.00000002505210838544172 + r * t;
    t = 0.0000002755731922398589 + r * t;
    t = 0.0000027557319223985893 + r * t;
    t = 0.0000248015873015873 + r * t;
    t = 0.0001984126984126984 + r * t;
    t = 0.001388888888888889 + r * t;
    t = 0.008333333333333333 + r * t;
    t = 0.041666666666666664 + r * t;
    t = 0.16666666666666666 + r * t;
    t = 0.5 + r * t;
    t = 1.0 + r * t;
    t = 1.0 + r * t;
    let mut y: f64 = t * 1.0;
    let mut j: i64 = 0;
    if k >= 0 {
        while j < k {
            y = y * 2.0;
            j = j + 1;
        }
    } else {
        while j < (0 - k) {
            y = y / 2.0;
            j = j + 1;
        }
    }
    return y;
}

fn dm_exp(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    if x >= 709.7827128933841 {
        return inf();
    }
    if x <= 0.0 - 745.1332191019412 {
        return 0.0;
    }
    return det_exp(x);
}

fn det_log_core(x: f64) -> f64 {
    let ln2: f64 = 0.6931471805599453;
    let mut k: i64 = 0;
    // f64-param-init: seed a loop-carried f64 binding via the exact identity
    // `x * 1.0` so mindc 0.10.2 does not infer it as i64.
    let mut m: f64 = x * 1.0;
    while m >= 1.4142135623730951 {
        m = m / 2.0;
        k = k + 1;
    }
    while m < 0.7071067811865476 {
        m = m * 2.0;
        k = k - 1;
    }
    let s: f64 = (m - 1.0) / (m + 1.0);
    let s2: f64 = s * s;
    let mut acc: f64 = 0.0;
    let mut n: i64 = 29;
    while n >= 1 {
        acc = 1.0 / (n as f64) + s2 * acc;
        n = n - 2;
    }
    return (k as f64) * ln2 + 2.0 * s * acc;
}

fn dm_log(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    if x < 0.0 {
        return 0.0;
    }
    if x == 0.0 {
        return 0.0 - inf();
    }
    if x >= inf() {
        return inf();
    }
    return det_log_core(x);
}

fn dm_sqrt(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if x < 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    return sqrt(x);
}

// erf by its Maclaurin series, ascending fixed left-fold.  Certified |x| < 2.
fn erf_series(x: f64) -> f64 {
    let two_over_sqrtpi: f64 = 1.1283791670955126;
    let x2: f64 = x * x;
    let mut t: f64 = x * 1.0;
    let mut s: f64 = x * 1.0;
    let mut n: i64 = 1;
    while n < 60 {
        t = t * (0.0 - x2) / (n as f64);
        s = s + t / ((2 * n + 1) as f64);
        n = n + 1;
    }
    return two_over_sqrtpi * s;
}

// erfc by a fixed-depth backward continued fraction.  Certified x >= 1.
// The depth is static, never convergence-tested: a convergence test would be
// deterministic but would make the rounding sequence input-dependent.
fn erfc_cf(x: f64) -> f64 {
    let sqrtpi: f64 = 1.7724538509055159;
    let mut acc: f64 = 0.0;
    let mut n: i64 = 200;
    while n >= 1 {
        acc = ((n as f64) * 0.5) / (x + acc);
        n = n - 1;
    }
    return det_exp(0.0 - x * x) / sqrtpi / (x + acc);
}

fn dm_erfc(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    if x >= inf() {
        return 0.0;
    }
    if x <= 0.0 - inf() {
        return 2.0;
    }
    if x < 0.0 {
        return 2.0 - dm_erfc(0.0 - x);
    }
    if x < 1.0 {
        return 1.0 - erf_series(x);
    }
    return erfc_cf(x);
}

// Standard normal CDF.  Computed as 0.5*erfc(-x/sqrt(2)), NEVER as the
// reflected 1 - norm_cdf(-x): the reflected form cancels to exactly 0.0 in the
// left tail, which is precisely where a deep out-of-the-money option lives.
// See the deep-OTM KAT below, where the true call premium is 4.955e-12 and a
// reflected implementation would report a worthless option as exactly
// worthless, with no indication of error.
fn dm_norm_cdf(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    let inv_sqrt2: f64 = 0.7071067811865476;
    return 0.5 * dm_erfc(0.0 - x * inv_sqrt2);
}

// Standard normal PDF, phi(x) = exp(-x^2/2)/sqrt(2*pi).  Needed by gamma,
// vega and theta.  1/sqrt(2*pi) is a rounded constant, not a division.
fn dm_norm_pdf(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    let inv_sqrt_2pi: f64 = 0.3989422804014327;
    return inv_sqrt_2pi * det_exp(0.0 - 0.5 * x * x);
}

// ============================ KERNEL END ===================================

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Black-Scholes-Merton scaffolding with a continuous dividend yield q.
//
// All inputs are f64 and all are REQUIRED to be finite with S > 0, K > 0,
// sigma > 0, T > 0.  Those preconditions are checked by `bs_valid` and every
// public function returns the canonical qNaN when they do not hold, rather than
// a plausible-looking number.  A silent numeric fallback on a degenerate input
// is how a risk bug reaches production wearing a valid-looking Greek — and at
// second order there is no screen to catch it.
//
//   d1 = (log(S/K) + (r - q + sigma^2/2) * T) / (sigma * sqrt(T))
//   d2 = d1 - sigma * sqrt(T)
//
// EVALUATION ORDER IS PART OF THE CONTRACT.  d1 is formed exactly as in
// `examples/quant/black_scholes`, with the numerator summed left to right
// before the single division.  Regrouping it is algebraically identical and
// numerically different, and would change the last bit of every sensitivity
// below.  Do not "simplify" these expressions.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn bs_valid(s: f64, k: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> i64 {
    if s != s {
        return 0;
    }
    if k != k {
        return 0;
    }
    if sigma != sigma {
        return 0;
    }
    if t != t {
        return 0;
    }
    if s <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if k <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if sigma <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if t <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}

fn bs_d1(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    let sq: f64 = sigma * dm_sqrt(t);
    return (dm_log(s / k) + (r - q + 0.5 * sigma * sigma) * t) / sq;
}

fn bs_d2(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    return bs_d1(s, k, r, q, sigma, t) - sigma * dm_sqrt(t);
}

// --- First-order quantities, recomputed here as PRIVATE helpers -------------
// These are NOT the deliverable of this module — `examples/quant/black_scholes`
// owns the first-order surface and is the public home of delta/gamma/vega.
// They are duplicated `fn` (not `pub fn`) purely because several higher-order
// forms are most honestly written as a scaling of vega or gamma, and because
// this example must stay self-contained (roadmap 17.1, see header).  Keeping
// them private means this file adds no second public definition of a
// first-order Greek that could drift from the one in black_scholes.

fn hlp_vega(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    let sqt: f64 = dm_sqrt(t);
    let d1: f64 = bs_d1(s, k, r, q, sigma, t);
    return s * dm_exp(0.0 - q * t) * dm_norm_pdf(d1) * sqt;
}

fn hlp_gamma(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    let sqt: f64 = dm_sqrt(t);
    let d1: f64 = bs_d1(s, k, r, q, sigma, t);
    return dm_exp(0.0 - q * t) * dm_norm_pdf(d1) / (s * sigma * sqt);
}

// ===========================================================================
// HIGHER-ORDER GREEKS.
//
// Every scaling convention below is PER 1.00 of the underlying variable, never
// per point or per day: vanna is per 1.00 of vol and 1.00 of spot, charm is per
// YEAR, veta is per 1.00 of vol per YEAR.  A desk wanting "per vol point per
// day" divides at the call site.  Baking a presentation convention into a
// numeric contract is how two systems that agree on the mathematics disagree on
// the number.
//
// SIGN CONVENTIONS ARE STATED, NOT ASSUMED.  Charm and veta are the two places
// the literature genuinely disagrees, because "time" can mean time-to-expiry T
// (increasing away from expiry) or calendar time t (decreasing toward it), and
// the two differ by a sign.  This module fixes:
//     charm = -d(delta)/dT        (delta decay per year of time PASSING)
//     veta  = +d(vega)/dT         (vega sensitivity per year of time TO EXPIRY)
// Both conventions were verified against a 50-digit central difference of the
// corresponding first-order Greek before the constants below were recorded; see
// the KAT header.  If you change a convention, change the KAT with it — a Greek
// whose sign convention is undocumented is worse than one that is absent.
// ===========================================================================

// --- VANNA: d(vega)/dS = d(delta)/dsigma -----------------------------------
//     vanna = -e^(-qT) * phi(d1) * d2 / sigma
// The cross-derivative, and the reason this file exists.  Note that it is
// exactly the same number whether you differentiate vega by spot or delta by
// vol (Schwarz/Clairaut), which is asserted as an identity in the KAT.  The
// `d2/sigma` factor is a small difference divided by a small number: this is the
// cancellation-sensitive term.
pub fn hog_vanna(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if bs_valid(s, k, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let d1: f64 = bs_d1(s, k, r, q, sigma, t);
    let d2: f64 = d1 - sigma * dm_sqrt(t);
    return 0.0 - dm_exp(0.0 - q * t) * dm_norm_pdf(d1) * d2 / sigma;
}

// --- VOLGA (vomma): d^2(price)/dsigma^2 = d(vega)/dsigma --------------------
//     volga = vega * d1 * d2 / sigma
// The convexity of the price in volatility.  `d1 * d2` changes sign around the
// at-the-money-forward point, and near that crossing the product is a
// difference of comparable magnitudes — a wrong erfc in the fifth digit moves
// volga's SIGN there, not merely its value.  A sign error in volga flips a
// long-convexity book to short with no visible symptom.
pub fn hog_volga(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if bs_valid(s, k, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let d1: f64 = bs_d1(s, k, r, q, sigma, t);
    let d2: f64 = d1 - sigma * dm_sqrt(t);
    return hlp_vega(s, k, r, q, sigma, t) * d1 * d2 / sigma;
}

// --- CHARM (delta decay): -d(delta)/dT, call ---------------------------------
//     charm_call = q*e^(-qT)*N(d1)
//                  - e^(-qT)*phi(d1) * (2*(r-q)*T - d2*sigma*sqrt(T))
//                    / (2*T*sigma*sqrt(T))
// The numerator `2*(r-q)*T - d2*sigma*sqrt(T)` is a genuine difference of two
// same-order terms, then divided by `2*T*sigma*sqrt(T)`, which goes to zero as
// expiry approaches.  Short-dated charm is therefore a small difference over a
// small number — the SHORT KAT case (one day to expiry) exists to pin exactly
// that regime.
pub fn hog_charm_call(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if bs_valid(s, k, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let sqt: f64 = dm_sqrt(t);
    let d1: f64 = bs_d1(s, k, r, q, sigma, t);
    let d2: f64 = d1 - sigma * sqt;
    let df_q: f64 = dm_exp(0.0 - q * t);
    let num: f64 = 2.0 * (r - q) * t - d2 * sigma * sqt;
    let den: f64 = 2.0 * t * sigma * sqt;
    return q * df_q * dm_norm_cdf(d1) - df_q * dm_norm_pdf(d1) * num / den;
}

// --- CHARM, put -------------------------------------------------------------
//     charm_put = -q*e^(-qT)*N(-d1)
//                 - e^(-qT)*phi(d1) * (2*(r-q)*T - d2*sigma*sqrt(T))
//                   / (2*T*sigma*sqrt(T))
// The second term is shared with the call, exactly as gamma is shared: the
// put-call parity difference is linear in S with a time-dependent coefficient,
// so only the N(.) term differs.  That gives the parity identity asserted in
// the KAT, which is the cheapest structural check on both signs at once.
pub fn hog_charm_put(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if bs_valid(s, k, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let sqt: f64 = dm_sqrt(t);
    let d1: f64 = bs_d1(s, k, r, q, sigma, t);
    let d2: f64 = d1 - sigma * sqt;
    let df_q: f64 = dm_exp(0.0 - q * t);
    let num: f64 = 2.0 * (r - q) * t - d2 * sigma * sqt;
    let den: f64 = 2.0 * t * sigma * sqt;
    return 0.0 - q * df_q * dm_norm_cdf(0.0 - d1) - df_q * dm_norm_pdf(d1) * num / den;
}

// --- VETA: d(vega)/dT -------------------------------------------------------
//     veta = -S*e^(-qT)*phi(d1)*sqrt(T)
//            * ( q + (r-q)*d1/(sigma*sqrt(T)) - (1 + d1*d2)/(2*T) )
// Vega decay.  The bracket subtracts `(1 + d1*d2)/(2T)` from two smaller terms,
// and `1 + d1*d2` is the same cancellation-prone product that drives volga and
// zomma, here offset by one.  Convention: per 1.00 of vol per YEAR of
// time-to-expiry (see the sign-convention note above).
pub fn hog_veta(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if bs_valid(s, k, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let sqt: f64 = dm_sqrt(t);
    let d1: f64 = bs_d1(s, k, r, q, sigma, t);
    let d2: f64 = d1 - sigma * sqt;
    let brk: f64 = q + (r - q) * d1 / (sigma * sqt) - (1.0 + d1 * d2) / (2.0 * t);
    return 0.0 - s * dm_exp(0.0 - q * t) * dm_norm_pdf(d1) * sqt * brk;
}

// --- SPEED: d(gamma)/dS = d^3(price)/dS^3 -----------------------------------
//     speed = -gamma/S * (d1/(sigma*sqrt(T)) + 1)
// Third order in spot.  A gamma-hedging desk uses speed to decide how fast the
// hedge goes stale; an error here shows up as a hedge that is rebalanced at the
// wrong frequency, which is a cost nobody attributes to a numeric bug.
pub fn hog_speed(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if bs_valid(s, k, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let sqt: f64 = dm_sqrt(t);
    let d1: f64 = bs_d1(s, k, r, q, sigma, t);
    return 0.0 - hlp_gamma(s, k, r, q, sigma, t) / s * (d1 / (sigma * sqt) + 1.0);
}

// --- ZOMMA: d(gamma)/dsigma -------------------------------------------------
//     zomma = gamma * (d1*d2 - 1) / sigma
// The `(d1*d2 - 1)` factor is the cancellation in its starkest form: at the
// point where d1*d2 = 1 the true zomma is exactly zero, and every bit of error
// in d1 or d2 lands directly on a quantity whose true value has no magnitude to
// hide it in.  This is where a non-deterministic erfc stops being an academic
// concern.
pub fn hog_zomma(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if bs_valid(s, k, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let d1: f64 = bs_d1(s, k, r, q, sigma, t);
    let d2: f64 = d1 - sigma * dm_sqrt(t);
    return hlp_gamma(s, k, r, q, sigma, t) * ((d1 * d2 - 1.0) / sigma);
}

// --- COLOR: d(gamma)/dT -----------------------------------------------------
//     color = -e^(-qT)*phi(d1) / (2*S*T*sigma*sqrt(T))
//             * ( 2*q*T + 1
//                 + (2*(r-q)*T - d2*sigma*sqrt(T))/(sigma*sqrt(T)) * d1 )
// Gamma decay.  Divided by `2*S*T*sigma*sqrt(T)`, so like charm it blows up
// near expiry — the SHORT KAT case puts color at roughly -69.6, four orders of
// magnitude above its one-year value, which is exactly the regime where an
// imprecise kernel does the most damage.
pub fn hog_color(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if bs_valid(s, k, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let sqt: f64 = dm_sqrt(t);
    let d1: f64 = bs_d1(s, k, r, q, sigma, t);
    let d2: f64 = d1 - sigma * sqt;
    let lead: f64 = 0.0 - dm_exp(0.0 - q * t) * dm_norm_pdf(d1) / (2.0 * s * t * sigma * sqt);
    let brk: f64 = 2.0 * q * t + 1.0 + (2.0 * (r - q) * t - d2 * sigma * sqt) / (sigma * sqt) * d1;
    return lead * brk;
}

// --- ULTIMA: d(volga)/dsigma = d^3(price)/dsigma^3 --------------------------
//     ultima = -vega/sigma^2 * ( d1*d2*(1 - d1*d2) + d1^2 + d2^2 )
// Third order in volatility, and the worst-conditioned quantity in this file.
// The bracket is a difference of products of d1 and d2, each of which is itself
// a quotient of logs and roots: the error in the kernel has been amplified
// three times over by the time it reaches this number.  It is also, by a wide
// margin, the one no human will ever sanity-check by inspection.
pub fn hog_ultima(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if bs_valid(s, k, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let d1: f64 = bs_d1(s, k, r, q, sigma, t);
    let d2: f64 = d1 - sigma * dm_sqrt(t);
    let brk: f64 = d1 * d2 * (1.0 - d1 * d2) + d1 * d1 + d2 * d2;
    return 0.0 - hlp_vega(s, k, r, q, sigma, t) / (sigma * sigma) * brk;
}

// ===========================================================================
// KNOWN-ANSWER TESTS.
//
// THE ORACLE.  Every reference value below was produced by an INDEPENDENT
// 50-digit oracle — mpmath 1.3.0 at `mp.dps = 50`, evaluating the closed forms
// with N(x) = erfc(-x/sqrt(2))/2 and phi(x) = exp(-x^2/2)/sqrt(2*pi) in
// 50-digit arithmetic, then rounded ONCE to f64.  Never by this implementation.
// A KAT generated from the code it tests proves only that the code is
// unchanged, not that it is correct.
//
// THE ORACLE WAS ITSELF VALIDATED, which matters more here than usual because a
// wrong reference is worse than no reference: at third order, "the formula I
// typed in" and "the derivative of Black-Scholes" are very easy to get apart,
// and a KAT locked to a mistyped formula would freeze the bug permanently.  So
// each closed form was cross-checked, offline at 50 digits, against a central
// difference of the corresponding lower-order Greek with h = 1e-20:
//     vanna  vs d(vega)/dS   AND  d(delta)/dsigma     (both, independently)
//     volga  vs d(vega)/dsigma
//     charm  vs -d(delta)/dT
//     veta   vs d(vega)/dT
//     speed  vs d(gamma)/dS
//     zomma  vs d(gamma)/dsigma
//     color  vs d(gamma)/dT
//     ultima vs d(volga)/dsigma
// All eight agreed with the closed form to 18 significant digits at all five
// KAT points.  That validation is the ONLY finite differencing anywhere in this
// example's provenance, it happened offline in the oracle, and none of it is in
// the shipped code.
//
// THE FIVE POINTS, AND WHY EACH ONE IS HERE:
//   ATM    S=100 K=100 r=0.05 q=0    sigma=0.20 T=1      — the textbook case.
//   DIV    S=100 K=100 r=0.05 q=0.03 sigma=0.25 T=2      — the ONLY case with
//          q != 0, so the only case where charm_call and charm_put differ at
//          all.  Without it the charm parity identity is vacuous: at q=0 both
//          charms are the same number and "call - put = 0" passes for free.
//   WING   S=50  K=100 r=0.05 q=0    sigma=0.20 T=0.25   — d2 = -6.856, five
//          standard deviations out.  Every Greek here is 1e-7 to 1e-11, formed
//          as differences of quantities of order 1.  This is where cancellation
//          is worst and where a reflected-identity erfc returns a hard zero.
//   SHORT  S=100 K=100 r=0.05 q=0    sigma=0.20 T=1/365  — one day to expiry.
//          charm, veta and color all carry a 1/T or 1/(T*sqrt(T)) factor, so
//          this is the blow-up regime: color is -69.6 here against -0.0105 at
//          one year, four orders of magnitude.
//   LOWVOL S=100 K=100 r=0.01 q=0    sigma=0.05 T=0.5    — the 1/sigma and
//          1/sigma^2 factors in vanna, volga, zomma and ultima amplify here:
//          ultima is -667.7 against -182.7 at 20 vol.
//
// THREE CLASSES OF CHECK, deliberately:
//   1. VALUE      — against the oracle, within a stated ulp band.
//   2. IDENTITY   — relations that must hold STRUCTURALLY regardless of the
//                   oracle, and which therefore survive a bad reference.  These
//                   are the real value of this file; see the block below.
//   3. ADVERSARIAL— a deliberately WRONG reference must be REJECTED.  A
//                   spurious acceptance is counted as a failure, which is what
//                   proves the bands discriminate rather than passing
//                   vacuously.  Without these the bands prove nothing.
//
// ON THE BANDS.  They are wider than the black_scholes KAT's, and that is a
// finding rather than a concession: each of these quantities is one or two
// derivatives further from the kernel, so the kernel's honest error against a
// 50-digit oracle is amplified by exactly the differentiation that defines the
// Greek.  The widest bands are at WING and SHORT, which is the whole point of
// including those points.  A band is stated per-check rather than globally so
// that the conditioning of each quantity is visible in the source.
//
// WHAT THIS KAT CANNOT CATCH, STATED PLAINLY.  These are tolerance bands, so
// they are structurally blind to any change that perturbs the result by less
// than the band — including a reassociation of an expression that is
// algebraically identical and numerically different.  Tightening the bands is
// not the fix: they must stay wide enough to absorb the honest difference
// between a re-derived erfc and the 50-digit oracle.
//
// Detecting reassociation requires a BIT-EXACT reference, which is the job of
// the cross-substrate CI fixture (tests/cross_substrate_identity.rs): it
// compares hashes across x86 `avx2` and ARM `neon`, and a hash has no tolerance
// to hide in.  This KAT proves the sensitivities are CORRECT; the fixture proves
// they are REPRODUCIBLE.  A green KAT is not evidence of byte-identity.
// ===========================================================================

fn chk_ulp(got: f64, want: f64, ulps: f64) -> i64 {
    let eps: f64 = 0.0000000000000002220446049250313;
    let tol: f64 = ulps * eps * absf(want);
    if absf(got - want) <= tol {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

fn chk_abs(got: f64, want: f64, abstol: f64) -> i64 {
    if absf(got - want) <= abstol {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

fn is_nan(x: f64) -> i64 {
    if x != x {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

pub fn main() -> i64 {
    let mut fails: i64 = 0;

    // T = 1/365, written as the f64 nearest the exact rational.  The oracle
    // used the exact rational 1/365 at 50 digits and rounded once; the tiny
    // difference between that and this literal is inside the stated band.
    let day: f64 = 0.0027397260273972603;

    // =====================================================================
    // 1. VALUE CHECKS against the 50-digit oracle.
    // =====================================================================

    // --- Case ATM: S=100 K=100 r=0.05 q=0 sigma=0.2 T=1 -------------------
    // Oracle vanna = -0.2814302601877034087806274...
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_vanna(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.0 - 0.2814302601877034, 1024.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_volga(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 9.850059106569619, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_charm_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.0 - 0.06566706071046413, 1024.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_charm_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.0 - 0.06566706071046413, 1024.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_veta(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 16.46367022098065, 1024.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_speed(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.0 - 0.0005159554770107896, 1024.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_zomma(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.0 - 0.08888505717594966, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_color(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.0 - 0.010530182235356568, 1024.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_ultima(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.0 - 182.68928077476355, 4096.0));

    // --- Case DIV: q=0.03, sigma=0.25, T=2 --------------------------------
    // The only q != 0 point: charm_call and charm_put differ here and only
    // here, which is what makes the charm parity identity below non-vacuous.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_vanna(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.03, 0.25, 2.0), 0.09170408337298644, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_volga(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.03, 0.25, 2.0), 0.0 - 3.7598674182924436, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_charm_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.03, 0.25, 2.0), 0.0 - 0.008761223584414772, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_charm_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.03, 0.25, 2.0), 0.0 - 0.037014159591942236, 1024.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_veta(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.03, 0.25, 2.0), 10.137759050101188, 1024.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_speed(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.03, 0.25, 2.0), 0.0 - 0.00018544603526537257, 1024.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_zomma(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.03, 0.25, 2.0), 0.0 - 0.041509343871652456, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_color(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.03, 0.25, 2.0), 0.0 - 0.0030671194884790086, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_ultima(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.03, 0.25, 2.0), 0.0 - 56.49753873500562, 4096.0));

    // --- Case WING: S=50 K=100 sigma=0.2 T=0.25, d2 = -6.856 --------------
    // Five standard deviations out.  Every value here is 1e-7 to 1e-11,
    // assembled from differences of order-1 quantities: this is the worst
    // cancellation in the file, and the bands say so honestly.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_vanna(50.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.25), 0.0000000016719700162319223, 16384.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_volga(50.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.25), 0.0000002824154568619661, 16384.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_charm_call(50.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.25), 0.0 - 0.0000000006931732889476093, 16384.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_veta(50.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.25), 0.00000011952367282471319, 16384.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_speed(50.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.25), 0.000000000012985595610577933, 16384.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_zomma(50.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.25), 0.000000002210553089986048, 16384.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_color(50.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.25), 0.0000000009171729306699607, 16384.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_ultima(50.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.25), 0.00006117873208772484, 16384.0));
    // The structural half of the wing claim: these are SMALL, not ZERO.  An
    // erfc built on the reflected identity 1 - N(-x) cancels to exactly 0.0 out
    // here, and a relative band against a tiny number can be argued with; a
    // strict inequality cannot.  Volga and zomma are positive at this wing
    // because d1*d2 = 46.3 is far above 1.
    if hog_volga(50.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.25) > 0.0 {
        fails = fails + 0;
    } else {
        fails = fails + 1;
    }
    if hog_zomma(50.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.25) > 0.0 {
        fails = fails + 0;
    } else {
        fails = fails + 1;
    }

    // --- Case SHORT: one day to expiry, the 1/T blow-up regime -------------
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_vanna(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, day), 0.0 - 0.015658567140361693, 8192.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_volga(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, day), 0.0015015064381168747, 8192.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_charm_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, day), 0.0 - 1.3335879681208043, 8192.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_veta(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, day), 380.8972554504883, 8192.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_speed(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, day), 0.0 - 0.010478191178092034, 8192.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_zomma(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, day), 0.0 - 1.9048516438190497, 8192.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_color(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, day), 0.0 - 69.56042469859834, 8192.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_ultima(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, day), 0.0 - 0.02824154124739208, 8192.0));

    // --- Case LOWVOL: sigma=0.05, the 1/sigma^2 amplification --------------
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_vanna(100.0, 100.0, 0.01, 0.0, 0.05, 0.5), 0.0 - 0.9749145966607301, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_volga(100.0, 100.0, 0.01, 0.0, 0.05, 0.5), 10.967789212433214, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_charm_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.01, 0.0, 0.05, 0.5), 0.0 - 0.06267308121390408, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_veta(100.0, 100.0, 0.01, 0.0, 0.05, 0.5), 27.149630598078726, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_speed(100.0, 100.0, 0.01, 0.0, 0.05, 0.5), 0.0 - 0.006128034607581732, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_zomma(100.0, 100.0, 0.01, 0.0, 0.05, 0.5), 0.0 - 2.184505064089079, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_color(100.0, 100.0, 0.01, 0.0, 0.05, 0.5), 0.0 - 0.11423909970156627, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hog_ultima(100.0, 100.0, 0.01, 0.0, 0.05, 0.5), 0.0 - 667.6761371244648, 4096.0));

    // =====================================================================
    // 2. MATHEMATICAL IDENTITIES.
    //
    // These are the real value of this file.  A value check confirms one number
    // against one oracle; an identity confirms a RELATION that must hold no
    // matter what the oracle says, and therefore survives a mistyped reference
    // — which, at third order, is the likeliest error of all.  Each identity
    // below was confirmed against the 50-digit oracle BEFORE being asserted
    // here, including its sign; none is assumed from memory.
    // =====================================================================

    // --- IDENTITY 1: vanna is a genuine cross-derivative -------------------
    //   vanna = d(vega)/dS = d(delta)/dsigma   (Clairaut/Schwarz symmetry)
    // Expressed without any finite difference: differentiating vega by S gives
    //   d(vega)/dS = -vega * d2 / (S * sigma * sqrt(T))
    // and differentiating delta by sigma gives the same thing.  If the mixed
    // partials disagree, either d1/d2 or the vega scaling is wrong — and a
    // vanna that is not a true cross-derivative silently breaks every
    // vol-spot correlation hedge built on it.
    //
    // This identity is checked at three points with different signs of vanna
    // (ATM negative, DIV positive, LOWVOL strongly negative) so it cannot pass
    // by coincidence of magnitude.
    let vn_a: f64 = hog_vanna(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0);
    let alt_a: f64 = 0.0 - hlp_vega(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0) * bs_d2(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0) / (100.0 * 0.2 * dm_sqrt(1.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(vn_a, alt_a, 0.0000000001));

    let vn_b: f64 = hog_vanna(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.03, 0.25, 2.0);
    let alt_b: f64 = 0.0 - hlp_vega(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.03, 0.25, 2.0) * bs_d2(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.03, 0.25, 2.0) / (100.0 * 0.25 * dm_sqrt(2.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(vn_b, alt_b, 0.0000000001));

    let vn_c: f64 = hog_vanna(100.0, 100.0, 0.01, 0.0, 0.05, 0.5);
    let alt_c: f64 = 0.0 - hlp_vega(100.0, 100.0, 0.01, 0.0, 0.05, 0.5) * bs_d2(100.0, 100.0, 0.01, 0.0, 0.05, 0.5) / (100.0 * 0.05 * dm_sqrt(0.5));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(vn_c, alt_c, 0.0000000001));

    // --- IDENTITY 2: charm put-call parity --------------------------------
    //   charm_call - charm_put = +q * e^(-qT)
    // Delta parity says delta_call - delta_put = e^(-qT); charm is -d/dT of
    // delta, and -d/dT of e^(-qT) is +q*e^(-qT).  The sign here was VERIFIED
    // against the oracle, not recalled: the intuitive guess -q*e^(-qT) is
    // wrong, and would have been frozen into this file undetected because at
    // q=0 both signs give zero.
    //
    // Checked at DIV (q=0.03), the only point where the two charms differ at
    // all — this is why the DIV case exists.
    let ch_c: f64 = hog_charm_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.03, 0.25, 2.0);
    let ch_p: f64 = hog_charm_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.03, 0.25, 2.0);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(ch_c - ch_p, 0.03 * dm_exp(0.0 - 0.06), 0.0000000001));
    // The positive control for the check above: the difference is a real,
    // non-zero number (0.02825...), so the assertion is not passing because
    // both sides are zero.  Without this line the q=0 degenerate below could
    // masquerade as a meaningful pass.
    if absf(ch_c - ch_p) > 0.01 {
        fails = fails + 0;
    } else {
        fails = fails + 1;
    }
    // And the degenerate direction: at q=0 the two charms must be EXACTLY
    // equal, because the only term that distinguishes them carries a factor q.
    let ch_c0: f64 = hog_charm_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0);
    let ch_p0: f64 = hog_charm_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(ch_c0 - ch_p0, 0.0, 0.0));

    // --- IDENTITY 3: volga is vega scaled by d1*d2/sigma -------------------
    //   volga = vega * d1 * d2 / sigma
    // and the companion relation for zomma,
    //   zomma = gamma * (d1*d2 - 1) / sigma.
    // Together these pin the sign structure of the vol-convexity surface: the
    // two differ by exactly the "-1", so volga and zomma must straddle zero at
    // DIFFERENT points (d1*d2 = 0 versus d1*d2 = 1).  A single sign error in
    // d2 would satisfy neither.
    let vg_a: f64 = hog_volga(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0);
    let vg_alt: f64 = hlp_vega(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0) * bs_d1(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0) * bs_d2(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0) / 0.2;
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(vg_a, vg_alt, 0.0000000001));

    let zm_a: f64 = hog_zomma(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0);
    let zm_alt: f64 = hlp_gamma(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0) * ((bs_d1(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0) * bs_d2(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0) - 1.0) / 0.2);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(zm_a, zm_alt, 0.0000000001));

    // The structural consequence, at ATM where d1*d2 = 0.0525: volga is
    // POSITIVE (d1*d2 > 0) while zomma is NEGATIVE (d1*d2 < 1).  This is the
    // straddling claim above, asserted rather than described.
    if vg_a > 0.0 {
        fails = fails + 0;
    } else {
        fails = fails + 1;
    }
    if zm_a < 0.0 {
        fails = fails + 0;
    } else {
        fails = fails + 1;
    }

    // --- IDENTITY 4: ultima is volga's derivative, scaled by vega ----------
    //   ultima = -vega/sigma^2 * ( d1*d2*(1 - d1*d2) + d1^2 + d2^2 )
    // Third order in vol.  Checked structurally against the same vega and the
    // same d1/d2 used by volga, so a drift between the two vol-convexity
    // Greeks cannot pass unnoticed.
    let ul_a: f64 = hog_ultima(100.0, 100.0, 0.01, 0.0, 0.05, 0.5);
    let u_d1: f64 = bs_d1(100.0, 100.0, 0.01, 0.0, 0.05, 0.5);
    let u_d2: f64 = bs_d2(100.0, 100.0, 0.01, 0.0, 0.05, 0.5);
    let ul_alt: f64 = 0.0 - hlp_vega(100.0, 100.0, 0.01, 0.0, 0.05, 0.5) / (0.05 * 0.05) * (u_d1 * u_d2 * (1.0 - u_d1 * u_d2) + u_d1 * u_d1 + u_d2 * u_d2);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(ul_a, ul_alt, 0.0000001));

    // --- IDENTITY 5: speed is gamma's derivative in spot -------------------
    //   speed = -gamma/S * (d1/(sigma*sqrt(T)) + 1)
    // and gamma is strictly positive, so at ATM (d1 = 0.35 > 0) speed must be
    // strictly negative: gamma falls as spot rises above the peak.
    let sp_a: f64 = hog_speed(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0);
    let sp_alt: f64 = 0.0 - hlp_gamma(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0) / 100.0 * (bs_d1(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0) / (0.2 * dm_sqrt(1.0)) + 1.0);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(sp_a, sp_alt, 0.0000000001));
    if sp_a < 0.0 {
        fails = fails + 0;
    } else {
        fails = fails + 1;
    }

    // =====================================================================
    // 3. DOMAIN CHECKS: degenerate inputs must return the canonical qNaN.
    //
    // Not a plausible-looking number.  Zero vol, an expired option, a
    // non-positive spot or strike are caller errors, and every one of the nine
    // functions says so with the SAME bit pattern — 0x7FF8000000000000, pinned
    // as a literal in the kernel block, because x86 and ARM disagree on the
    // sign bit of a synthesised NaN and a quiet divergence there would break
    // byte-identity on exactly the error path nobody tests.
    // =====================================================================

    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hog_vanna(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hog_volga(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hog_charm_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hog_charm_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hog_veta(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hog_speed(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hog_zomma(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hog_color(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hog_ultima(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0)));

    // Expired: T = 0.  Every 1/T and 1/sqrt(T) factor would otherwise produce
    // an infinity dressed as a risk number.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hog_vanna(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hog_volga(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hog_charm_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hog_charm_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hog_veta(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hog_speed(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hog_zomma(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hog_color(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hog_ultima(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.0)));

    // Non-positive spot and strike.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hog_vanna(0.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hog_volga(100.0, 0.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hog_ultima(0.0 - 100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hog_color(100.0, 0.0 - 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    // Negative vol and negative time.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hog_zomma(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.0 - 0.2, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hog_speed(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.0 - 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hog_veta(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.0 - 0.05, 0.25)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hog_charm_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.0 - 0.5)));

    // =====================================================================
    // 4. ADVERSARIAL NEGATIVE CONTROLS.
    //
    // Each line below asks the band to accept a value that is WRONG, and adds
    // the result straight into `fails` — so a spurious acceptance IS a failure.
    // This is the only thing that makes the bands above mean anything: without
    // it, every value check could be passing because the tolerance is wide
    // enough to admit anything, and the file would be a green test that cannot
    // go red.  The perturbations are chosen just large enough to be outside the
    // stated band and far smaller than anything a human would notice by eye,
    // which is the realistic failure mode for a third-order Greek.
    // =====================================================================

    // Vanna off in the 4th digit (-0.2814 -> -0.2824).  A vol-spot hedge sized
    // on this would be 0.4% wrong and nothing on any screen would show it.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(hog_vanna(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.0 - 0.2824302601877034, 1024.0);
    // Volga off in the 4th digit (9.8500 -> 9.8600).
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(hog_volga(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 9.860059106569619, 4096.0);
    // Ultima off by 0.1 in 182.7 — under a tenth of a percent.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(hog_ultima(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.0 - 182.78928077476354, 4096.0);
    // A SIGN error on charm, the classic convention bug: if the band accepted
    // this, the file's stated sign convention would be unenforced.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(hog_charm_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.06566706071046413, 1024.0);
    // A flush-to-zero wing value must FAIL the wing band.  This is the
    // adversarial form of the left-tail claim: it proves the WING checks above
    // can actually catch a reflected-identity erfc, rather than passing because
    // a relative band around 1e-9 is easy to satisfy.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(0.0, 0.0000002824154568619661, 16384.0);
    // And the corresponding trap for the strict-positivity checks: zomma at the
    // wing must be rejected if it is reported as exactly zero.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(0.0, 0.000000002210553089986048, 16384.0);

    return fails;
}
